sections.5.body:
Both are time trackers, but they're built around different jobs.

**Toggl Track is a strong choice if:**

- people need to track time from phones or desktop apps;
- you need timesheet approvals;
- you rely on integrations such as Jira or Asana;
- up to 3 people only need basic tracking, and the free plan covers it.

**Paidlyo is a strong choice if:**

- you bill clients by the hour and want invoices created from unbilled time in one click;
- you want rates per project and task, budgets and outstanding invoices in one place;
- you'd rather pay one simple price, $9 per person a month or $90 per person a year, with every feature included;
- your team works on computers, so a browser-based tool is enough.

Paidlyo has no free plan, no mobile apps, no approvals and no integrations. It does have a Harvest CSV importer, but no Toggl importer, so moving from Toggl means starting fresh with clients, projects and rates.

If you're already on Toggl and the invoicing step is the problem, see our page on [Paidlyo as a Toggl Track alternative](/alternatives/toggl-track). For a side-by-side with Harvest, read [Harvest vs Toggl Track](/compare/harvest-vs-toggl-track).

**Clockify fits well if:**

- you want the lowest price per seat, or a free plan for a small team;
- you need approvals, time off, GPS, scheduling, expenses or kiosks;
- you want a QuickBooks integration or mobile apps;
- you're comfortable choosing between five plans as your needs change.

**Paidlyo fits well if:**

- you bill clients by the hour and want invoices created from unbilled time in one click;
- you want budgets, rates per project and task, and outstanding invoice reports without moving up a tier;
- you prefer one price, $9 per person a month or $90 per person a year, with every feature included;
- your team works on computers, so a browser-based tool is enough.

Paidlyo has no free plan, no mobile apps, no approvals, no expenses and no integrations. It imports from Harvest, but not from Clockify, so switching means setting up clients, projects and rates again.

For a direct comparison with Harvest, see [Harvest vs Clockify](/compare/harvest-vs-clockify), which looks at the costs for a 10-person team by need.
